      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named after the biblical port of Jaffa, reflecting hopes for a thriving harbor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joppa%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1768, the county seat moved from Joppa to Baltimore as the harbor silted and diseases spread.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joppa%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Joppa hosts the preserved Benjamin Rumsey House, the only surviving 18th‑century building from its colonial era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Joppa%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Joppatowne, a planned community built in 1961, grew around Joppa and now forms part of the modern suburb.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
